---
/* Privacy policy. Every statement here mirrors what the app (src-tauri) and this static
 * site actually do; change this page with them. */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { project } from '../lib/distribution';
const updated = { iso: '2026-11-12', label: 'November 12, 2026' };
const sections = [
  ['overview', 'The short version'],
  ['app', 'The desktop app'],
  ['automatic', 'Automatic requests'],
  ['connect', 'Connect and the mobile app'],
  ['providers', 'Services you choose'],
  ['website', 'This website'],
  ['children', 'Children'],
  ['changes', 'Changes to this policy'],
  ['contact', 'Contact'],
] as const;
---
<Page title="Privacy Policy" current="privacy" eyebrow="YOUR WORK. YOUR BUSINESS."
  heading="Privacy, plainly put."
  description="PandaCode collects nothing: no license checks, no telemetry, no analytics and no crash reporting. Your work stays on your computer. PandaCode Connect, which links the mobile app to your computers, is optional and keeps only what it needs to find your devices."
  lede="PandaCode is free, open-source software that runs on your computer. The only server we run is the optional Connect relay, and it can’t read your work. Here is exactly what the apps, the relay and this website do.">
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <div class="legal-summary page-rise">
      <div><b>We collect nothing.</b><span>No license checks. Connect, if you use it, keeps only your device list.</span></div>
      <div><b>No telemetry.</b><span>No analytics, usage tracking or crash reporting. None.</span></div>
      <div><b>Open to inspect.</b><span>Every line is on GitHub, so you can check all of this yourself.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="legal-updated page-rise">Last updated <time datetime={updated.iso}>{updated.label}</time></p>
  </Fragment>

  <div class="legal-layout section-width">
    <nav class="legal-toc" aria-label="On this page"><p>ON THIS PAGE</p><ol>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>
    <article class="legal-prose">
      <section id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
        <h2 id="overview-title">The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Without Connect, PandaCode collects nothing.</strong> Unless you sign in to PandaCode Connect, there are no accounts, no license checks, no telemetry, no analytics, no crash reporting and no PandaCode servers.</li>
          <li><strong>PandaCode Connect</strong> (optional, for the mobile app and for running tasks on another computer) keeps your GitHub account id and your devices’ names and public keys. Everything you send through it is <strong>end-to-end encrypted</strong>; we can’t read it. See <a href="#connect">Connect and the mobile app</a>.</li>
          <li>Your projects, conversations, tasks, bots, memories and settings are stored <strong>on your device</strong>. We never receive a copy.</li>
          <li>The app talks only to the services <strong>you choose</strong>, directly from your computer, plus a few automatic requests that carry no personal data.</li>
          <li>This website is static. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics and loads nothing from other companies.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="app" aria-labelledby="app-title">
        <h2 id="app-title">The desktop app</h2>
        <p>PandaCode keeps its data in your operating system’s application data folder: a local database with your projects, conversations, tasks, board, team settings, bots and their memories, and usage figures, plus cached files such as screenshots you attach. Passwords, API keys, bot tokens and cloud credentials you enter are kept in a private file readable only by your user on macOS, and in the operating system’s secure store elsewhere (Credential Manager on Windows, Secret Service on Linux).</p>
        <p>None of this leaves your computer unless a service you use needs it (see <a href="#providers">Services you choose</a>). Uninstalling the app and deleting its data folder removes it. Unsent message drafts (below) live in the app’s web storage, which is inside the data folder only on Linux; on Windows and macOS deleting the data folder leaves them.</p>
        <p>If PandaCode crashes, it writes a plain-text crash report to the same folder. A report holds the panic message, if any, which can include file paths, text from your work or a credential that was in that message (nothing is redacted), and a backtrace, so check it before you share it. Reports stay on your computer and are never sent; the 10 newest (plus up to 5 reports about the app’s window) are kept, and reports older than 90 days are deleted when a new report is written, each time PandaCode starts and about once a day while it runs, so one stays longer only while PandaCode is not running. A small file there, <code>crash-guard.json</code> (<code>crash-guard-serve.json</code> for a headless server), holds the times of the crashes in the 10 minutes before the latest one (at most 10; a time more than 10 minutes old is dropped at the next crash, when PandaCode or a headless server next starts and about once a day while one runs, so it stays longer only while PandaCode is not running) and, until the next launch of that app or server picks it up, a short notice: when it happened, a cause such as “Panic” or “Crashed with SIGSEGV”, whether PandaCode restarted, whether running agents were stopped, and the file name of its report. A notice whose crash is more than 90 days old (or dated more than a day ahead) is deleted at those same starts and daily checks. A headless server (<code>pandacode serve</code>) that restarts after a crash writes all its output, including its startup line about the crash (which ends with the full path of the crash reports folder, naming your home folder), your Connect GitHub login and error messages, which can name threads and contain project and file paths, to <code>serve-restart.log</code> in the same folder, readable only by your user on macOS and Linux (on Windows it gets the permissions of the profile folder it is in); once it passes 1 MB it moves to one older file (checked every 30 seconds on macOS and Linux, so it can pass 1 MB by what is written in that time, and on Windows only when a restart begins). The restart guard’s own few lines go to <code>crash-guard.log</code> there (moved to one older file past 1 MB when the guard starts). These logs are deleted once they haven’t been written for 90 days, checked when PandaCode starts and about once a day while it runs; a restarted server never deletes its own <code>serve-restart.log</code>, so that one goes at a later start. The restart guard runs as a second PandaCode process whose command line, which other users of the computer can see in the process list while PandaCode runs, includes the data folder’s path and PandaCode’s own arguments.</p>
        <p>Text you type in the message box (or a shared bot channel’s) and haven’t sent yet (up to 50,000 characters per draft) is saved as plain text in the app’s local web storage for up to 30 days, so a restart usually doesn’t lose it (the web view may write it to disk slightly later, so the very last keystrokes before a hard crash can be missing). A draft can include a secret you pasted. Turn off Settings → General → <em>Keep unsent drafts</em> (on by default) to save none: that removes the saved drafts at once and at every launch, and text in an open message box stays only until you quit. <em>Clear saved drafts</em> removes the drafts kept so far and keeps saving new ones (text showing in an open message box stays there until its next change). Older text is cleared when PandaCode starts and daily while it runs, so it stays longer only while PandaCode is not opened. On Linux that storage is in the data folder, which PandaCode makes readable only by your user each time it starts. On Windows it is in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\ai.pandacode.desktop</code>, outside the data folder; on macOS WebKit normally keeps it in <code>~/Library/WebKit/ai.pandacode.desktop</code>. On Windows and macOS PandaCode changes no permissions there, so who can read your drafts depends on what the web view and the parent folders allow. Delete those folders too to remove drafts.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="automatic" aria-labelledby="automatic-title">
        <h2 id="automatic-title">Automatic requests</h2>
        <p>The app makes a few requests on its own. They carry no content from your work and no identifier for you, only what any internet request includes, such as your IP address:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Agent version checks</strong>, about every 6 hours, so PandaCode can tell you when your coding agents have updates: Homebrew (formulae.brew.sh), Anthropic (downloads.claude.ai), GitHub (api.github.com), Kimi (cdn.kimi.com) and npm (registry.npmjs.org, for Cline).</li>
          <li><strong>PandaCode’s own updates</strong>, at launch and about every 6 hours: the latest release’s update manifest on GitHub (github.com). Updates are signed and verified before they install. Choose Automatic, Notify me or Off in Settings → About.</li>
          <li><strong>A public model price list</strong> from GitHub (raw.githubusercontent.com), at most once a day, to estimate costs.</li>
          <li><strong>The model routing profiler</strong> (about 35 MB) from GitHub (raw.githubusercontent.com), once, verified before use. It is fetched the first time you turn on a team (with model routing on), or when you click Download or Route in Settings, never at launch. Routing then runs on your computer.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you open the usage panel, or Settings → Providers when account pools are shown, the app asks your providers for your usage limits with the sign-in those tools already saved on your computer (in Settings at most every 5 minutes, or when you click Refresh). The email address and plan shown for each pooled account are read from those tools’ own login files on your computer and never leave it. These requests go straight to those companies. PandaCode never writes Claude Code’s sign-in. If Claude Code’s usage can’t be read when it is checked (for example, its saved sign-in has expired), PandaCode can run your own Claude Code once (claude -p /usage, a local command: no model call, nothing billed) which lets Claude Code refresh and save its sign-in itself if it needs to, just as when you open it; Claude Code then contacts Anthropic for that, with its telemetry, error reports and other nonessential traffic turned off for this run. This is on by default and runs at most once every 15 minutes per Claude setup (less often while it keeps failing, and never while your Keychain is locked or while a rate limit’s server-set wait lasts); turn it off in Settings → Providers → Claude Code → Let PandaCode ask Claude Code for usage when it can’t read it, and such a check then shows the last saved usage until you open Claude Code. Links you click open in your browser or in the built-in browser, and those websites make their own connections.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="connect" aria-labelledby="connect-title">
        <h2 id="connect-title">Connect and the mobile app</h2>
        <p>PandaCode Connect is optional and off until you sign in, in the desktop app (Settings → Remote access) or in the PandaCode mobile app for iPhone and Android. It links your own devices so you can follow and answer tasks from your phone, or run a task on another of your computers. The mobile app never runs code; your computer does the work.</p>
        <p>You sign in with GitHub. The sign-in asks GitHub for no permissions beyond who you are: no email, no repositories. Our relay, a Cloudflare Worker, then keeps:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>your <strong>GitHub numeric user id</strong>, which names your account. At sign-in the relay also reads your GitHub login and avatar URL and passes them to your device, which shows them in Settings;</li>
          <li>for each device you sign in: a <strong>random device id</strong> the app makes, the <strong>device name</strong> and platform, its <strong>public key</strong>, whether it accepts remote control, and when it was last online.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>That is used only to make Connect work: to let your devices find each other. It is linked to your account, never used for tracking or advertising, never sold and never shared.</p>
        <p><strong>Your work is end-to-end encrypted.</strong> Tasks, prompts, replies and approvals travel between your devices encrypted with keys that never leave them (X25519 and XChaCha20-Poly1305). The relay forwards the ciphertext and stores none of it. We can’t read it, and neither can Cloudflare.</p>
        <p>Like any web service, the relay sees your IP address. It uses it to rate-limit sign-ins, and Cloudflare may keep standard request logs to run and secure the service. We don’t use them to identify or track you.</p>
        <p>The mobile app has no analytics, advertising, tracking or crash reporting, and it contacts only GitHub (to sign in, and to load your avatar) and the relay. Messages you write offline, your snoozes and pins, and your sign-in stay on the phone in its secure storage.</p>
        <p><strong>Push notifications</strong> are optional and off until you allow them in the mobile app. The app then gives the relay your phone’s push token, and the relay sends generic alerts (such as “A task finished”, with a link to the inbox) through Apple Push Notification service or Firebase Cloud Messaging. No task content is sent.</p>
        <p><strong>Deleting your account.</strong> In the mobile app, open Settings → Delete account. This removes your account and every device on it from the relay at once, and signs your devices out. Removing a single device from the device list deletes that device’s row. Deleting the account doesn’t change your GitHub account; you can also revoke PandaCode’s access in GitHub under Settings → Applications → Authorized OAuth Apps.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="providers" aria-labelledby="providers-title">
        <h2 id="providers-title">Services you choose</h2>
        <p>PandaCode is a workspace for tools you bring. When you use one, the app sends it what the work needs, directly from your computer, under your own account with that service and its privacy policy.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Coding agents and model providers</strong>, such as Claude Code, Codex, Kimi Code, OpenCode, Antigravity, Grok, Devin, Hermes, Cursor, GLM, MiMo and Alibaba Token Plan, receive your prompts, the files and context they read, attachments and tool output.</li>
          <li><strong>GitHub</strong>, through the <code>gh</code> command-line tool you sign in to, when you link pull requests or use GitHub features.</li>
          <li><strong>Bots</strong> run on your computer. Messages you exchange through Telegram, Slack, Discord or WhatsApp pass through those platforms. Web chat is served from your own computer.</li>
          <li><strong>Virtual machines and cloud sandboxes.</strong> Local VMs run on your Mac. Daytona, Cloudflare and AWS sandboxes run in your own accounts and receive the commands, files and screen interactions you or your agents send.</li>
          <li><strong>Deploys</strong> to Vercel, Cloudflare, Netlify or AWS run their command-line tools in your project with your credentials and upload what you deploy. Saving a Netlify token checks it once with Netlify (api.netlify.com).</li>
          <li><strong>Jev</strong> (optional, off by default): decision assistance sends selected task excerpts, rules and saved diffs to TypeSafe, or to OpenRouter (openrouter.ai) with your key if you choose it there (opening its model menu fetches the public model list, without a key); browser testing sends test goals and visible page content to TypeSafe and your configured text model provider.</li>
          <li><strong>Voice input</strong> is optional, and audio leaves your device only for the destination you choose. With <em>This device</em>, a speech model you downloaded transcribes on your computer and audio is never sent anywhere. With a speech service, only when you stop or transcribe a recording (Stop, Enter, Transcribe recording, Retry) or when switching threads or anything else closes the prompt box mid-recording while PandaCode keeps running, PandaCode sends recorded audio, the chosen speech model and optional language directly to the transcription endpoint you configured, with your speech API key if needed. Automatic stops (the two-minute or 16 MiB limit, or a failing microphone) send nothing and keep the audio until you choose Transcribe recording. It makes no voice requests, microphone access or model downloads at launch, while browsing settings or when saving profiles. Keys stay in native secret storage and are never returned to the webview. Failed audio is held only in bounded memory for Retry and cleared when Retry succeeds, when you discard it, choose Record again or delete the thread the dictation started in, or when PandaCode quits. The microphone test plays back on your device and is never transcribed or stored.</li>
          <li><strong>Local speech models</strong> are downloaded from Hugging Face (huggingface.co, *.huggingface.co and the *.hf.co download servers) only when you click Download in the voice settings: the model you pick and, the first time, a small voice-activity model. Each file is pinned to a fixed version and checked against its published checksum. Nothing is downloaded at launch or while you browse the model list.</li>
          <li><strong>Downloads you start</strong>, such as VS Code themes from Open VSX, or Tart and other tools from GitHub.</li>
          <li><strong>Skills you add</strong> in Settings → Tools → Skills: only when you click Find skills, choose a folder or click Install, <code>npx</code> fetches the <code>skills</code> command-line tool from the npm registry set in your <code>~/.npmrc</code> (registry.npmjs.org unless you changed it), which reads the source you chose, with its telemetry turned off: a repository it downloads from GitHub or GitLab, or a folder on your computer. Listing skills and switching them off stay on your computer.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="callout">Agents act with the permissions you give them and can send your data to other services as part of a task. Review what you allow, especially with full access.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="website" aria-labelledby="website-title">
        <h2 id="website-title">This website</h2>
        <p>This website is static. It has no cookies, analytics, advertising, social widgets, remote fonts or tracking pixels, and it loads nothing from other companies. Downloads come from GitHub Releases, where GitHub’s own privacy statement applies.</p>
        <p>Like any website, our web host may keep standard server logs, such as IP addresses, browser details and the pages requested, to keep the site running and secure. We don’t use them to identify or track you.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="children" aria-labelledby="children-title">
        <h2 id="children-title">Children</h2>
        <p>PandaCode is a developer tool and isn’t directed to children. We hold nothing about anyone beyond the Connect details above, which are a GitHub account id and device names.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="changes" aria-labelledby="changes-title">
        <h2 id="changes-title">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>We’ll update this page if PandaCode changes and show the date of the last update at the top. Because PandaCode is open source, every change to the app is public on GitHub.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
        <h2 id="contact-title">Contact</h2>
        <p>Questions about privacy, or help deleting Connect data? Open an issue in <a href={project.issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub issues</a>. To report a security problem privately, follow our <a href={project.security} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">security policy</a> (SECURITY.md in the repository).</p>
        <p>See also the <a href="/license/">PandaCode License</a> and <a href="/support/">Support</a> for the mobile app.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Page>
